new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..e6f56a0b68
--- /dev/null
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommbe/patches-6.18/0505-02-net-ethernet-qualcomm-ppe-offload-vlan-aware-bridges.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,1113 @@
+From: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
+Date: Fri, 03 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload VLAN-aware bridges
+
+Extend the bridge offload to VLAN-aware bridges. Each VLAN of an
+offloaded bridge is backed by its own VSI: ingress VLAN translation
+rules assign tagged packets of the member ports and untagged packets
+of the PVID ports to the VSI, and the per-VSI egress tag modes provide
+tagged or untagged egress per port, with the CPU port always tagged so
+the software ports of the bridge see the VLAN of flooded packets. The
+per-port default C-VID supplies the VLAN ID when a packet received
+untagged on a PVID port leaves through a tagged port.
+
+The translation rules match by ingress port bitmap, so they scale with
+the number of VLANs rather than ports times VLANs: all member ports of
+a VLAN share one rule, and the ports with a common PVID share another.
+Ports of a VLAN-aware bridge drop packets that miss the translation
+rules, which implements the ingress side of VLAN filtering. Running
+out of the 64 VSIs or the 128 rules fails the VLAN object, falling
+back to software for that VLAN only; toggling vlan_filtering on an
+offloaded bridge stays refused in both directions.
+
+PVID changes are transactional through the final default-CVID write;
+failure restores the old translation rule and software state.
+
+The VSI state moves from the port to the VSI table: memberships,
+untagged and PVID port bitmaps live per VSI now, FDB events carry the
+VLAN ID and resolve their VSI through it, and the FDB synchronization
+reports learned entries with the VLAN ID of their VSI.
+
+Signed-off-by: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
+---
+--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_bridge.c
++++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_bridge.c
+@@ -4,13 +4,16 @@
+ 
+ /* switchdev based offload of software bridges to the PPE L2 bridge.
+  *
+- * Each offloaded software bridge is backed by a VSI (virtual switch
+- * instance) of the PPE. PPE ports joined to a VLAN-unaware bridge are
+- * bound to its VSI, which makes the hardware learn, age and forward
+- * within the VSI autonomously. The CPU port is a member of every VSI,
+- * so that unknown unicast, multicast and broadcast packets are flooded
+- * to the CPU and reach software ports (such as wireless interfaces) of
+- * the bridge.
++ * Offloaded software bridges are backed by VSIs (virtual switch
++ * instances) of the PPE, within which the hardware learns, ages and
++ * forwards autonomously. PPE ports joined to a VLAN-unaware bridge
++ * are bound directly to its single VSI. On a VLAN-aware bridge each
++ * VLAN is backed by its own VSI, and ingress VLAN translation rules
++ * assign the packets of the member ports to it, with the egress tag
++ * modes of the VSI providing tagged or untagged egress per port. The
++ * CPU port is a member of every VSI, so that unknown unicast,
++ * multicast and broadcast packets are flooded to the CPU and reach
++ * software ports (such as wireless interfaces) of the bridge.
+  */
+ 
+ #include <linux/etherdevice.h>
+@@ -31,12 +34,30 @@
+ 
