--- /dev/null
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommbe/patches-6.18/0507-01-net-ethernet-qualcomm-ppe-offload-netfilter-flowtables.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,1262 @@
+From: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
+Date: Sat, 04 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: offload netfilter flowtables
+
+Add the PPE resource manager and rule translator used by netfilter
+flowtable offload.  A later patch publishes it through the PPE port
+netdevices' ndo_setup_tc callback.
+
+An offloaded rule maps to a next hop entry carrying the egress port,
+the destination MAC rewrite, the egress VLAN of a VLAN push action and
+the NAT addresses, plus a flow entry carrying the 5-tuple and the
+translated L4 port. The router MAC table is maintained from the source
+MAC rewrite of the rules: the egress source MAC of one direction is
+the MAC the reverse direction ingresses on, so a connection offloaded
+in both directions installs all the router MACs it needs. The egress
+L3 interfaces, indexed by port, provide the source MAC rewrite and the
+routing enable of each port, keyed off the same rewrite action.
+
+Only IPv4 TCP and UDP flows with at most one NAT direction are
+offloaded; everything else, including VLAN-tagged ingress, stays on
+the software flowtable path, as do flow misses of offloaded
+connections. Public SNAT addresses and router MACs are refcounted
+scarce resources (16 and 8 entries); exhaustion falls back to
+software. Both tables are backed by a common refcounted-table helper,
+struct ppe_ft_ref, which keeps the entry scan and the reference
+bookkeeping in one place. Before a flow is inserted, the host table
+entry of its source is added or referenced and its hardware index
+is copied into the flow. It is deleted only with the last referencing
+flow, after that flow has been deleted. Flow statistics
+come from the per-flow hit counters, keeping the flowtable entries
+alive while traffic flows in hardware.
+
+Teardown explicitly drains rules which survive flow-block unbind
+through the ordinary destroy transaction. This keeps hardware deletion
+and every resource release in the normal FLOW_CLS_DESTROY path.
+
+A repeated FLOW_CLS_REPLACE for an already-installed cookie reports
+success without touching hardware. The core re-sends REPLACE from
+flow_offload_refresh() for as long as a flow's packets traverse the
+software path, so treating it as a fresh installation would program a
+duplicate row for the same tuple and leak a next hop slot on every
+refresh until the table is exhausted.
+
+Tuple masks and mangle offsets are validated against forms the hardware can
+represent.  Ethernet rewrite admission intentionally follows the forms seen
+from netfilter, including partial or duplicate L2 rewrites; unsupported
+mixed-NAT rules still fall back to software.
+
+A flow whose hardware delete fails retains its host, next hop and address
+table slots. The driver clears VALID as a best-effort containment step and
+keeps the indexed flow on a quarantine list until driver teardown, rather
+than forgetting a row which may still forward.
+
+Signed-off-by: Kenneth Kasilag <kenneth@kasilag.me>
